Java 8
ava 8 is the 8th version of Java, released in 2014.

Before Java 8, Java programs were more lengthy (boilerplate code).

Java 8 introduced new features to make code:

=> Shorter
=>Easier to read
=> Easier to write
=>Better performance for many tasks

Why Java 8 is used?

Suppose you want to sort students.

Before Java 8

You create a separate comparator class.

class NameComparator implements Comparator<Student> {

    @Override
    public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
        return s1.name.compareTo(s2.name);
    }
}

Then

Collections.sort(list, new NameComparator());

More code.

After Java 8

Using a Lambda Expression:

Collections.sort(list, (s1, s2) -> s1.name.compareTo(s2.name));

 Same work.

 Less code.

Runnable r = new Runnable() {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
};

Very lengthy.

Java 8
Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Hello");

Only one line.

Important Features of Java 8
1. Lambda Expression 

Used to reduce code.

Example:

(a, b) -> a + b
2. Stream API 

Used for filtering, sorting, counting, mapping collections easily.

Example:

list.stream()
    .filter(x -> x > 10)
    .forEach(System.out::println);
3. Method Reference

Instead of

list.forEach(x -> System.out.println(x));

Write

list.forEach(System.out::println);
4. Functional Interface

Interface with only one abstract method.

Example:

@FunctionalInterface
interface Test {
    void display();
}
5. Default Methods

Interfaces can now have implemented methods.

interface Demo {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}
6. Optional Class

Helps avoid NullPointerException.

7. Date and Time API

New classes like:

LocalDate
LocalTime
LocalDateTime


